\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around three analyses: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2, including the inconclusive layer effect C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 are primary supported claims; C3 (layer effect) is inconclusive; C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, conditional OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity (\S\ref{sec:method}). A Type III sum-of-squares decomposition\footnote{Type III SS avoids ordering dependence in our unbalanced design; logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant results (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).} partitions variance as: clause identity 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), model 1.0\%, condition 15.8\%, residual 60.8\% (of which $\sim$15\% is stochastic and $\sim$46\% systematic, estimated from an OpenAI-family ICC replication; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). A permutation test confirms that clause wording---not type membership---drives this variance ($p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_variance}). The incompleteness effect ($+$49.2\,pp) is an upper bound that includes information addition; controlling for token count changes no type's predicted effect by more than 0.1\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). The confound-free range is $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$36.0\,pp (authorization scope); excluding incompleteness entirely, the pooled effect is $+$31.0\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$).

Replication on two additional models confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); SOPBench~\citep{li_sopbench_2025} confirms directional consistency ($+12.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy predicts failure mechanisms substantially more strongly than violation rate (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$; the raw NMI ratio is inflated by target cardinality asymmetry; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

