\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Differential nocuity and intervention limits.}
Our results support a \emph{differential nocuity} hypothesis \citep{berry_kamsties_2004,willis_nocuous_2008}: the binary effect is domain-robust ($p = 0.187$) but type-specific profiles require per-domain calibration: cross-domain type ranking is near chance ($\rho = 0.26$, $p = 0.62$), driven by conditional precedence ($\Delta = 22.0$\,pp retail vs.\ airline); deployment in other domains requires independent type-effect calibration. Of the 60.8\% unexplained variance, $\sim$15\% is stochastic and $\sim$46\% systematic (within-provider ICC $= 0.749$; cross-provider consistency supported by C1 replication across seven models; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). The explained share (39.2\%) is comparable to behavioral research norms; the large systematic residual likely reflects clause$\times$context interactions invisible to type-level analysis, further supporting clause-level over type-level auditing as the actionable intervention. Even perfect disambiguation would address at most 22.4\% of variance; prompt-based mitigation is type-dependent (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}), reinforcing policy-design over prompt-level fixes.

\paragraph{Taxonomy as diagnostic tool.}
Type membership explains only 0.4\% of violation variance versus 22.4\% for clause wording; however, it predicts failure \emph{mechanism} substantially more strongly than violation rate (Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), validating the taxonomy as a diagnostic tool (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

\paragraph{Ecological validity.}
Our synthetic design establishes causal mechanisms but overestimates naturalistic effect sizes. The SOPBench replication ($+$12.6\,pp under constraint-level measurement; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}) confirms directional consistency; absolute effect calibration requires grain-matched benchmarks.
